WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.443
Roman Germany, around AD 325.

00:00:02.453 --> 00:00:05.215
You're sealing a bottle of wine.

00:00:05.315 --> 00:00:06.867
It's not for drinking.

00:00:06.877 --> 00:00:08.642
It's for the dead, for their journey

00:00:08.652 --> 00:00:09.938
to the afterlife.

00:00:09.948 --> 00:00:11.399
A man and a woman, in two

00:00:11.409 --> 00:00:13.895
stone coffins, near today's Speyer.

00:00:13.905 --> 00:00:15.494
To keep the air out, you pour

00:00:15.504 --> 00:00:17.119
a thick layer of olive oil on

00:00:17.129 --> 00:00:17.808
top.

00:00:17.818 --> 00:00:18.200
1867.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:19.966
Diggers open the tomb.

00:00:19.976 --> 00:00:22.615
Of all the glass vessels, only one

00:00:22.625 --> 00:00:24.344
still holds liquid.

00:00:24.459 --> 00:00:25.649
This is the real one:

00:00:25.659 --> 00:00:27.384
the Speyer wine bottle.

00:00:27.396 --> 00:00:29.299
Dolphin handles, about one and a half

00:00:29.309 --> 00:00:30.108
litres.

00:00:30.118 --> 00:00:31.761
It has never been opened.

00:00:31.771 --> 00:00:32.699
Inside:

00:00:32.709 --> 00:00:35.074
what's left of a herb-infused wine, under

00:00:35.084 --> 00:00:37.384
a solid layer of old olive oil.

00:00:37.394 --> 00:00:40.190
Since 2024, it's not the oldest wine.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.996
A Roman urn in Carmona, Spain, holds

00:00:43.006 --> 00:00:46.363
older wine, mixed with its owner's ashes.

00:00:46.463 --> 00:00:48.332
But it's still a bottle nobody dares

00:00:48.342 --> 00:00:49.318
to open.

00:00:49.355 --> 00:00:51.518
Sealed back in Roman Germany...
